  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 138 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nA mid/upper ridge axis extended from the Lower Mississippi Valley \narea NNW across the Central Plains while a stationary front was \nbisecting Kansas early this afternoon. Low clouds and fog were \nobserved north of the front impacting much of northern and western \nKansas. This frontal boundary is progged to meander over the area \nthrough tonight before mixing northward as we move into the day on \nFriday. This is expected to result in another round of low ceilings \nand areas of fog developing along and north of the boundary again \ntonight. We may also see a sharp temperature gradient, especially \nwhere low clouds persist. Some elevated showers and thunderstorms \nwill be possible across parts of central and northeast Kansas this \nevening and tonight, aided by a developing LLJ. PWATs are progged to \nrise to 1.25+ inches supporting efficient rainfall. We could see a \nstrong storm or two with gusty winds and hail given elevated CAPE \nabove 1500 J/KG this evening. The initial development could be over \ncentral KS before the activity focuses further north across \nnortheast KS where we will see a higher potential for some pockets \nof excessive rain.\n\nA brief lull is expected late tonight in early Friday over southern \nKS while some storms linger across northeast KS early on Friday. \nIncreasing probabilities for rain will return as we move through the \nday as a negatively tilted shortwave trough lifts northeast towards \nthe area. With steep mid-lvl lapse rates progged early in the \nperiod, some embedded thunderstorms are also possible at times, \nespecially across northern KS later in the day. Some drier air could \nbe wrapped in southeast KS, limiting rainfall as we move into the \nevening and overnight hours as the system quickly lifts north of the \narea. Some low pops were maintained on Sat but lingering clouds \nshould keep temperatures near climate normals with highs mostly in \nthe 60s.\n\nShortwave ridging will return to the area on Sunday with mostly dry \nweather conditions anticipated during the day. With some breaks in \nthe clouds, afternoon highs may rise into the lower 70s in some \nareas while parts of central and western KS will see more seasonable \nhighs in the 60s. Another robust mid/upper trough is progged to \nbuild over the Rockies late Sunday/Sunday night. Showers and \nthunderstorms are expected to become more numerous Sunday night as \nthe exit region of the upper jet builds over the Southern Plains. \nThe pattern may support a few strong/severe storms as we move \nthrough the day on Monday but given the potential for more \nwidespread activity and potential training, pockets of heavy rain \nmay be a bigger concern. High pops were maintained Mon night into \nTuesday as numerous showers are anticipated under the upper low. A \ncold front will usher in more seasonable air as we move into Wed-Thu \nwith highs in the low to mid 60s. Some low pops were maintained on \nWed due to uncertainty in the timing of the mid/upper trough. Dry \nweather conditions are expected to return by late Wed into Thu.",
